From 4a8b32279314ef44ceef20e4e0829230dd024c6c Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Tue, 18 Aug 2026 14:20:57 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 2/2] Give every event page a way in for the next organizer
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

An organizer shares a link to their own event page, so that is where most
visitors meet this site — and that page said nothing about the fact that anyone
could be running one. The home page's pitch is never seen by the people best
placed to act on it.

So every event page now ends with a short invitation to the organizer pitch.
It is the last thing on the page, after everything that helps somebody buy a
ticket, and it is deliberately the quietest panel there: the page belongs to
the organizer who built it, and this must never pull attention off their sale.
It uses the neutral surface rather than the per-event accent, because it is the
platform speaking rather than the event.

"Organizer sign in" was not that door either — it leads to a password box,
which is a dead end for anyone who does not have an account yet. The two are
now distinct: sign in for people who already work with us, the pitch for people
deciding whether to.

In the header, "Browse events" was a dim text link next to a real button, on
the one bar that tells an arriving visitor the rest of the site exists. It is a
filled green button now — green so it never reads as the ticket button that
owns every event page.

That second button then broke the header on phones: both labels wrapped onto
two lines each, and once they were stopped from wrapping the row needed 411px
of a 320px screen and cut the sign-in button off at the edge. Below 460px the
wordmark gives way instead — the flag mark still identifies the site and still
links home, the name is repeated in the page and the footer, and shortening the
labels would have left the only navigation reading "Browse" and "Sign in".

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
